# ── required ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Model access, via OpenRouter. Any structured-output-capable model works; the
# default (deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731 on the CLI, the undated
# deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash alias on the web route, reasoning off, fastest
# hosts pinned) was chosen by bake-off — five configs, one company, one table:
# the DeepSeek row returned 449 entities for $0.29 where the frontier-priced
# entrant returned 315 for $1.93, i.e. ~$0.001 per entity. The engine names the
# model on every span it bills.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Web access, via Bright Data. The token comes from Account settings → API keys
# on the SAME account that owns the zones below. SERP zone accepts a
# comma-separated list and the client rotates across them.
BRIGHTDATA_API_TOKEN=
BRIGHTDATA_SERP_ZONE=
BRIGHTDATA_UNLOCKER_ZONE=

# ── optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Basic auth for the hosted web app. Unset = the app is open.
KB_USER=
KB_PASSWORD=

# Read-only demo. `1` turns it on. POST /api/map refuses with a friendly
# sentence instead of starting a run, and the gallery serves the six maps
# committed under demo/maps/ instead of runs/. Every read route is untouched, so
# the maps, the graph, the notes and the export all work.
#
# This is what makes a public URL safe to hand to strangers: a live run through
# the app was measured at $0.147-$0.202 and 166-255 seconds over six hosted
# runs, and the shipped defaults already stop one at $0.41 — but auth and a
# ceiling only bound who and how much, and a visitor still waits three minutes
# for a map somebody has to pay for. Demo mode hands them six finished ones
# instead. Unset = the app builds maps as it always has.
#
# OPENKB_RUNS_DIR is NOT consulted in demo mode — that one says where new runs
# are written, and a demo writes none. To serve a different set of maps, point
# OPENKB_DEMO_MAPS_DIR at the directory. Unset, the app finds demo/maps/ itself.
OPENKB_DEMO=
OPENKB_DEMO_MAPS_DIR=

# Where new runs are written. next.config.ts fills this in with <repo>/runs when
# it is unset, which is why a local clone never has to set it — and why Vercel
# does: the repo root is read-only there, so DEPLOY.md makes
# OPENKB_RUNS_DIR=/tmp/openkb-runs mandatory on that host. The Dockerfile
# hard-codes /app/runs instead.
OPENKB_RUNS_DIR=

# The spend brake. The web app refuses to start a run once the OpenRouter key's
# cumulative usage since BASE exceeds the ceiling. Set BASE to the key's usage
# at deploy time (DEPLOY.md shows the one-line read); unset = no ceiling.
OPENKB_CEILING_USD=
OPENKB_CEILING_BASE_USD=

# Model override for the CLI scripts. LEFT COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE: this one
# is read with `?? "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731"`, and an empty assignment
# is not the same as unset — `set -a && source .env` exports the empty string,
# which reaches the provider as a model id of "" and fails the first call.
# Uncomment only with a real id. Same for the four swarm model dials below.
# OPENKB_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731

# ── the run's own spend caps (optional; both are ON by default) ──────────────
#
# THESE ARE NOT the deployment's OPENKB_RUN_CAP_USD below, and the names differ
# deliberately: the documented way to run a script is `set -a && . ./.env`, so
# a deployment's $0.41 sitting in this file would abort every terminal run in
# its first minute.
#
# A watchdog reads the run's live meter and aborts at the cap, writing
# runs/stopped-*.json. The default cap is $8.00 a run and it TRIPS at $6.00 —
# the cap less the reserve core holds back so the ending can still be written.
# The worst measured healthy run is $3.736 (scripts/spend-caps.ts), so the trip
# clears real work by 61%. A DEARER MODEL BREAKS THAT
# ARITHMETIC: set this to about 4x what you would otherwise expect, or to the
# word `off`. $50.00 per `pnpm batch` list, same reading rules.
OPENKB_CLI_RUN_CAP_USD=
OPENKB_BATCH_CAP_USD=

# Run persistence. Without these, runs are files under runs/ and a crashed run
# loses whatever it had not finished. With them, spans are written as they
# happen, so a dead run stays readable and a restarted server can still serve
# it. Schema: scripts/supabase-schema.sql
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# ── the deployment's budget (optional; a clone already ships it on) ──────────
#
# The web app's four limits, all defaulted in code so a clone with no .env is
# still bounded: $0.41 a map (derived from what the host's clock affords, not a
# literal), $5.00 a day, 3 maps per visitor per day, 3 at a time. The word
# `off` is the only spelling that turns one off. DEPLOY.md explains each.
OPENKB_RUN_CAP_USD=
OPENKB_DAY_CAP_USD=
OPENKB_RUNS_PER_VISITOR_PER_DAY=
OPENKB_RUNS_AT_ONCE=

# Let strangers spend: a whole number of runs a day the demo will buy for
# visitors. Unset is zero is closed — the limit IS the switch, so there is no
# state where runs are allowed and no cap is set. Roughly $0.20 a run.
OPENKB_PUBLIC_RUNS_PER_DAY=

# Salt for the per-visitor hash. Set it to anything on a shared host.
OPENKB_VISITOR_SALT=

# ── extra pipeline stages (optional; every one is OFF by default) ────────────
#
# Four A/B flags on the sweep. Each adds one model stage, each fails open — a
# call that throws leaves the map exactly as it was — and each writes its own
# counters into the run's report. `1` turns one on; anything else, unset
# included, is the unchanged default. Numbers below are from
# runs/sweep-cursor-com-20260821105321.json, which ran all four.

# Asks a model, from search titles and descriptions alone, which hosts deserve
# a fetch and a judgement at all. Skip is its only power. Buys the fetches and
# classify calls it declines: 123 of 926 hosts skipped, $0.009 in triage calls.
OPENKB_TRIAGE=

# Spends one more free fetch on each host the judge left `unknown` — a deeper
# page the search itself surfaced, not the front page — and re-asks the same
# classify question. Buys back hosts the front page did not explain: 22 asked,
# 13 rescued.
OPENKB_SECOND_LOOK=

# Gives every host settled `relation: "none"` one further batched opinion, from
# stored evidence only, before it ships dropped. On that run it confirmed all
# 27 and rescued none — which is the result worth knowing.
OPENKB_DROP_CONFIRM=

# Pulls the vendor names a roundup-shaped result already printed in its own
# title or description but that this run never searched for, and feeds them
# through the ordinary fold and judge — no second path onto the map.
OPENKB_LISTICLE_HARVEST=

# ── sweep tuning (optional; measured defaults ship in code) ──────────────────

# Result pages read per query. The CLI defaults to 4. NOTE what that costs: the
# library opens a query at 2 pages and lets `assess` promote a product to 4 on
# real page-2 yield, and since the deep depth is floored at the shallow one,
# setting 4 here reads four pages everywhere and leaves that promotion nothing
# to buy. Set it to 2 for the variable-depth behaviour the web route gets.
OPENKB_PAGES=

# Widening rounds fired before the model's "enough" is accepted. The CLI floors
# it at 3; one early "enough" ended a run at 36 queries where its twin ran 87.
# `0` restores the model's own judgement. An EMPTY value here reads as 0, not
# as unset — write a number or delete the line.
# OPENKB_MIN_WAVES=3

# The estimation stop: distinct hosts after which the search seals, whatever
# the planner wants. Unset ~900, which lands near ~700 kept nodes.
OPENKB_MAX_HOSTS=

# `1` skips the paid link pass entirely — the free title-and-description pass
# still runs, so the map keeps its measured edges and loses the inferred ones.
OPENKB_SKIP_MODEL_LINKING=

# `agent` runs phase one as the discovery agent — it pulls its own product
# pages and a `group` call splits them into markets — instead of the single
# understand call. Anything else, unset included, is the default.
OPENKB_DISCOVERY=

# Where the prompts are read from. Unset = the repo's prompts/.
OPENKB_PROMPTS_DIR=

# `bounded` turns DeepSeek's reasoning on. The bake-off measured that at $1.02
# against $0.29, 1424s against 229s, and 272 hosts left `unknown` against 77.
# Off is the default for those three numbers.
OPENKB_DEEPSEEK_REASONING=

# Per-model-call timeout in ms; 120000 default.
OPENKB_CALL_TIMEOUT_MS=

# How many corroborating searches a blocked host needs before it earns one
# ~$0.008 unlocker retry during rank. 3 default, `0` turns the escalation off —
# and an EMPTY value here reads as 0, so write a number or delete the line.
# OPENKB_RANK_UNLOCK=3

# ── speed dials (optional; measured defaults ship in code) ───────────────────

# Width of the rank pool — how many hosts are judged at once. 8 is the proven
# default; 24 measured ~3x the phase throughput with no provider pushback.
OPENKB_RANK_CONCURRENCY=

# Width of the SERP wave. 20 default; 32 measured fine on one zone — but two
# CONCURRENT runs share the zone, and a saturated wave reads as an empty round.
OPENKB_SEARCH_CONCURRENCY=

# Swarm lanes (investigators in flight). 6 is the design's number; 10 measured.
OPENKB_SWARM_LANES=

# The family floor: the fixed deck of market questions the swarm opens with.
# Unset keeps the library default (on, at 4); `off` disables it; 1-5 sizes it.
OPENKB_SWARM_FAMILIES=

# Swarm wall clock in ms (default 600000).
OPENKB_SWARM_WALL=

# Per-tier model overrides. COMMENTED OUT for the reason OPENKB_MODEL is: these
# are read with `?? "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731"`, so an empty assignment
# reaches the provider as a model id of "".
# OPENKB_SWARM_LEAD_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731
# OPENKB_SWARM_PEEK_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731
# OPENKB_SWARM_READ_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731
# OPENKB_SWARM_DIG_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731

# `1` prints the full error and stack when a CLI script dies, instead of the
# one-line diagnosis and its remedy.
OPENKB_TRACE=
